    {
      "id": "ledger-notes",
      "kind": {
        "text": "WHY THE SUFFICIENCY OF CHECKING F IS NOT AN ORACLE. `bcnf_violations` examines only the seven dependencies of F, which is sound because if every X -> Y in F with Y not contained in X has X+ = R, then any implied non-trivial A -> B has B inside A+ and B not inside A, so growing A+ used some L -> R of F with L inside A+, whence A+ contains L+ = R. That argument is in the module's documentation and is the one assumption in the footprint that is a theorem rather than a measurement.\n\nADR-0455 AND ADR-0460. Minimality here is absolute, and it is absolute for a stronger reason than 'every test returned a verdict'. ADR-0460's failure mode -- a decided test that is really a test of the producer's decomposition -- is structurally absent: attribute closure is defined by F alone, with no monomial order, no block decomposition, no budget and no heuristic anywhere in it. And the evidence for each negative verdict is not the closure computation but a two-row relation, an object that refutes superkey-hood on its own terms without mentioning closure at all.",
        "type": "prose"
      },
